<h1>AI Opportunity Pipeline</h1>
<p class="eyebrow">AI Strategy &middot; Case study</p>
<nav class="tabs" aria-label="AI Opportunity Pipeline sections"><a class="tab" href="/pipeline/overview.html">Overview</a><a class="tab" href="/pipeline/">Prototype</a><a class="tab" href="/pipeline/prd.html" aria-current="page">PRD</a></nav>

<p class="lead-sub">An intake, assessment, and decision register for AI ideas across a ten-college district. Requirements and a working draft, in development with the domain. It has taken no live requests.</p>

<div class="meta">
  <div><div class="l">Version</div><div class="v">0.1</div></div>
  <div><div class="l">Status</div><div class="v status">In development, with the domain for review</div></div>
  <div><div class="l">Owner</div><div class="v">Michelle Blomberg</div></div>
  <div><div class="l">Updated</div><div class="v">October 2026</div></div>
</div>

<h2>1. Summary</h2>
<p>The AI Opportunity Pipeline is a front door for AI ideas and a consistent way to decide what happens to each one. It is designed to collect what colleges already have, take requests for what they want, assess each entry on the same dimensions, and record the decision with a written reason. The working draft holds the request form, a list where submitted requests will appear (none has been received), and the start of an inventory with three records from real reporting. Assessing requests, the dashboard and the decision log are designed and not yet built. It has not launched, is not yet in testing, and has not taken a live request. The domain is refining it, and testing begins once the domain approves it.</p>

<h2>2. Goal</h2>
<p>Every AI idea that reaches the Student Support and Success domain leaves with an answer and a reason, reached the same way each time.</p>
<p>Three rules follow from that goal.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Inventory comes before requests. The cheapest fix is usually something another college already runs.</li>
  <li>&ldquo;Not an AI problem&rdquo; is a real answer. A process, policy, or staffing matter is documented and routed to whoever owns it.</li>
  <li>Declines are recorded as visibly as approvals.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3. Users and context</h2>
<p>Two groups use it. Student services staff and leaders at the ten Maricopa colleges submit what they have and what they need. Most are advisors and support staff with no time for a long form, so the form takes a few minutes and accepts &ldquo;I do not know&rdquo; as a useful answer. Members of the domain assess what comes in and record the decision.</p>
<p>The context is a district where the same service carries a different name at every college, where several colleges may license the same product without knowing it, and where there is no shared place to ask for help with AI.</p>

<h2>4. How it works</h2>
<h3>The submission form</h3>
<p>The form starts from the problem, not the technology, and it doubles as the notes page for a discovery meeting. A department is not expected to know what AI can do. It asks six things: what the office is trying to get done, what is getting in the way, how the work is done today, the people (who does it, who is affected and who approves a change), how often it happens and how long it takes, and what better would look like. The walk through the current process is how an AI problem is told apart from a process problem. Naming the kind of help is optional: a tool, an agentic workflow, training, a look at the process, or not sure. The assessment, not the requester, says whether AI fits.</p>
<h3>Track one, what exists</h3>
<p>Each reported tool or automated workflow is logged with its college, category, license, and how it is reported to be working. Each ends at one of five answers: share across colleges, consolidate to a district license, replace with something built, retire, or leave as is.</p>
<h3>Track two, what is wanted</h3>
<p>Requests for a tool, an agentic workflow, training, or a process review. Each ends at one of four answers: pilot, discovery, not an AI problem, or declined. This track opens after the inventory is out. In the working draft, a submitted request is saved and listed as new. The dispositions are designed and not yet built.</p>
<h3>Assessment</h3>
<p>Six dimensions structure the conversation: student impact, staff time returned, readiness, risk, feasibility, and scale. They are a conversation structure, not a formula, and the working draft describes them. It does not yet record an assessment against them. This working draft is the source of truth for the dimensions until the revisions in section 10 are made.</p>
<h3>Dashboard and decision log</h3>
<p>Designed, and not in the working draft. The dashboard would show college coverage, products duplicated across colleges, how tools are licensed and what kinds of tools exist. The decision log would list every assessed entry with its answer and the reason.</p>

<!-- prd-req:acc -->
<h3>Requirements and acceptance criteria</h3>
<p>Each requirement is one behavior. The checks beside it are what a builder or tester confirms before that requirement is called done. Status reflects what this document records as built.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Area</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Done when</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>R1</td><td>The submission form</td><td>The product must present a request form that asks the six problem-first questions: what the office is trying to get done, what is getting in the way, how the work is done today, who does the work and who is affected and who approves a change, how often it happens and how long it takes, and what better would look like.</td><td><ul><li>The form displays all six questions, in the order listed, each with its own answer field.</li><li>No question asks the requester to name a technology, a product or an AI capability.</li><li>A completed form can be submitted and the submitted answers match what was typed, question by question.</li></ul></td><td>Drafted, with the domain for review</td></tr>
<tr><td>R2</td><td>The submission form</td><td>The product must offer an optional kind of help field with five choices: a tool, an agentic workflow, training, a look at the process, or not sure.</td><td><ul><li>The field shows exactly the five choices named in section 4.</li><li>A form submitted with no choice selected is accepted and saved.</li><li>A form submitted with a choice selected shows that choice on the saved request.</li></ul></td><td>Drafted, with the domain for review</td></tr>
<tr><td>R3</td><td>The submission form</td><td>The user must be able to answer any question with I do not know and still submit the form without help.</td><td><ul><li>A form with I do not know entered in every one of the six questions is accepted and saved.</li><li>The form shows no error or warning that treats I do not know as an invalid answer.</li><li>A staff member who has not seen the form before completes and submits it with no assistance in an observed test, and the time taken is recorded.</li></ul></td><td>To verify</td></tr>
<tr><td>R4</td><td>Track two, what is wanted</td><td>The product must save each submitted request and show it in the list of requests received with the status new.</td><td><ul><li>Immediately after submission, the request appears in the list of requests received.</li><li>The request is shown with the status new.</li><li>The request is still in the list after the page is closed and reopened in the same browser.</li></ul></td><td>Drafted, with the domain for review</td></tr>
<tr><td>R5</td><td>Track one, what exists</td><td>The product must hold an inventory in which each reported tool or automated workflow is logged with its college, category, license, and how it is reported to be working.</td><td><ul><li>The inventory screen lists three records in the working draft.</li><li>Each record displays a value for college, category, license, and how it is reported to be working.</li><li>Each of the three records can be traced to a real report supplied by the product owner, and none is sample data.</li></ul></td><td>Drafted, with the domain for review</td></tr>
<tr><td>R6</td><td>Track one, what exists</td><td>The product must let a domain member close each inventory record with one of five answers: share across colleges, consolidate to a district license, replace with something built, retire, or leave as is.</td><td><ul><li>An inventory record offers exactly the five answers named in section 4.</li><li>Only one answer can be recorded on a record at a time.</li><li>The recorded answer is displayed on the inventory record after saving.</li></ul></td><td>Designed, not built</td></tr>
<tr><td>R7</td><td>Track two, what is wanted</td><td>The product must let a domain member close each request with one of four answers: pilot, discovery, not an AI problem, or declined.</td><td><ul><li>A request offers exactly the four answers named in section 4.</li><li>Recording an answer changes the request status from new to that answer in the list of requests received.</li><li>A request recorded as not an AI problem also records the owner it was routed to.</li></ul></td><td>Designed, not built</td></tr>
<tr><td>R8</td><td>Track two, what is wanted</td><td>The product must keep the request track closed to live requests until the inventory has been published.</td><td><ul><li>Before the inventory is published, the request form is not offered to college staff as a live intake.</li><li>After the inventory is published, the request form is available to student services staff and leaders at the ten colleges.</li><li>The date the inventory was published and the date the request track opened are both recorded.</li></ul></td><td>To verify</td></tr>
<tr><td>R9</td><td>Assessment</td><td>The product must let a domain member record an assessment of an entry against the six dimensions: student impact, staff time returned, readiness, risk, feasibility, and scale.</td><td><ul><li>An assessment record has one entry field for each of the six dimensions, labeled with the names used in section 4.</li><li>A saved assessment is attached to its request or inventory record and can be reopened and read.</li><li>The assessment record displays no computed total, rank or formula result, because the dimensions are a conversation structure and not a formula.</li></ul></td><td>Designed, not built</td></tr>
<tr><td>R10</td><td>Assessment</td><td>The product must check each proposed tool for privacy, security and accessibility as three pass or fail gates before any assessment against the dimensions is recorded.</td><td><ul><li>Each proposed tool has three separate gate results, each recorded as pass or fail.</li><li>An assessment against the six dimensions cannot be saved until all three gates are recorded.</li><li>A tool that fails any one gate is marked as not proceeding and receives no assessment against the dimensions.</li></ul></td><td>Designed, not built</td></tr>
<tr><td>R11</td><td>Dashboard and decision log</td><td>The product must require a written reason whenever an answer is recorded on a request or an inventory record.</td><td><ul><li>An answer cannot be saved while the reason field is empty.</li><li>The saved reason is displayed next to the answer on the entry.</li><li>The same rule applies to a declined request as to a pilot.</li></ul></td><td>Designed, not built</td></tr>
<tr><td>R12</td><td>Dashboard and decision log</td><td>The product must provide a decision log that lists every assessed entry with its answer and the written reason.</td><td><ul><li>Every entry that has an answer appears in the decision log, and no entry without an answer appears.</li><li>Each row shows the entry, the answer and the reason.</li><li>Declined entries appear in the same list and with the same fields as approved entries, with no filter that hides them by default.</li></ul></td><td>Designed, not built</td></tr>
