<h2>5. Data, privacy, and governance</h2>
<ul>
  <li>The working draft holds three inventory records, all from real reporting.</li>
  <li>It collects no student data. The form asks about a department&rsquo;s problems, tools and processes, not for any student record.</li>
  <li>A submission in the prototype is saved only in the submitter&rsquo;s own browser. Nothing is sent to a server.</li>
  <li>A launched version would hold staff names, departments, and decisions, so it needs sign-in, access control, and an audit trail. The prototype has none of these, which is one reason it is not launched.</li>
  <li>Proposed tools would be checked for privacy, security, and accessibility as pass-or-fail gates before any scoring. See section 10.</li>
</ul>

<h2>6. Build and portability</h2>
<ul>
  <li>One self-contained HTML file. Vanilla JavaScript, no framework, no backend, no account.</li>
  <li>No AI model and no API key. People do the assessing.</li>
  <li>Hosted on GitHub Pages.</li>
  <li>The operating model is the portable part. In an enterprise information technology environment this function lives in the service management platform, as a catalog item with a workflow, an assessment record, and a reporting layer. ServiceNow, TeamDynamix, and Jira Service Management all do this. The prototype demonstrates the model, not the tooling.</li>
</ul>

<!-- prd-req:dep -->
<h3>Dependencies</h3>
<p>What has to be in place, and who provides it.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Needed</th><th>Provided by</th><th>Needed for</th></tr></thead>
