<tr><td>One settled set of assessment criteria, including the revisions listed in section 10</td><td>Student Support and Success domain, with the AI director in district information technology who reviewed the model</td><td>R9, R10 and the first pilot step</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eight to ten real past requests together with the decision that was made on each</td><td>To be assigned</td><td>The calibration step in the pilot plan, before R9 is finalized</td></tr>
<tr><td>A platform decision: build on the prototype, or configure the model in a service management platform the district already owns</td><td>District information technology, decision maker to be named</td><td>R6, R7, R9, R11, R12, R13 and R15</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sign-in, access control and an audit trail</td><td>To be assigned after the platform decision</td><td>R15 and any launch</td></tr>
<tr><td>Members of the domain available to assess entries and record decisions</td><td>Student Support and Success domain</td><td>R6, R7, R9 and R11</td></tr>
<tr><td>Static hosting on GitHub Pages</td><td>Product owner</td><td>R16</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<!-- /prd-req:dep -->

<h2>7. Pilot and testing</h2>
<p>Testing happens before launch, in this order.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Settle one set of assessment criteria. Earlier drafts described the dimensions three different ways. The prototype&rsquo;s six are the starting point.</li>
  <li>Revise the model from the September 2026 review by an AI director in district information technology. The specific changes are in section 10.</li>
  <li>Calibrate on eight to ten real past requests. Score each one after the fact and compare the result to the decision that was made. Each disagreement is either a flaw in the model or a decision worth revisiting.</li>
  <li>Build the assessment record and the request dispositions into the prototype, or configure them in a district platform.</li>
  <li>Run the inventory round with the ten colleges.</li>
  <li>Open the request track to a small number of real requests, and take at least one through to a decline.</li>
</ol>

<h2>8. Definition of success</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Every entry leaves with a disposition and a written reason.</li>
  <li>On the calibration set, the model&rsquo;s result and the real decision agree, or the disagreement is explained.</li>
  <li>A department can complete the form in a few minutes without help.</li>
  <li>A request cannot become a pilot unless the requester names one measure and its current baseline.</li>
  <li>A decline is accepted because the criteria were published before the request arrived.</li>
  <li>The platform question is answered: build on this prototype, or configure the model in a system the district already owns.</li>
</ul>

<h2>9. Rollout</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Phase one.</strong> Inventory of what already exists at each college.</li>
  <li><strong>Phase two.</strong> The request track, opened after the inventory is published.</li>
  <li><strong>Out of scope.</strong> Building the solutions, making purchasing decisions, and anything that turns out not to be about AI. Those are routed to their owners.</li>
</ul>

<!-- prd-req:oos -->
<h3>Out of scope</h3>
<ul>
<li>Building the solutions that a request leads to.</li>
<li>Making purchasing decisions.</li>
<li>Resolving anything that turns out not to be about AI, such as a process, policy or staffing matter; these are documented and routed to their owners.</li>
<li>Collecting any student record or other student data.</li>
<li>Using an AI model to assess entries; people do the assessing.</li>
<li>Asking the requester to decide whether AI fits the problem; the assessment decides that.</li>
<li>Taking live requests before the domain approves the model. The product has not launched and is not yet in testing.</li>
<li>Serving as the long-term tooling; the prototype demonstrates the operating model, and the platform decision is still open.</li>
</ul>
<!-- /prd-req:oos -->

<h2>10. Open questions and risks</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Gates before scores.</strong> Privacy, security, and accessibility should be pass or fail, checked before anything is scored. A tool that fails one does not get a middling score. It does not proceed.</li>
  <li><strong>Scale is a multiplier.</strong> Scored beside the other dimensions, a district-wide low-value item outranks a high-value single-college one. Value should be impact multiplied by reach.</li>
  <li><strong>Risk should score its worst axis.</strong> A tool that is fine on six kinds of risk and severe on one should not average out to medium.</li>
  <li><strong>No measure of effort.</strong> The model can rank but cannot sequence. Adding a simple size estimate would let priority account for effort.</li>
  <li><strong>Missing dimensions.</strong> Strategic alignment and urgency are not assessed.</li>
  <li><strong>Four dispositions are too few.</strong> Missing: route to something the district already has, refer to another owner, defer with a named blocker and a revisit date, and what happens after a pilot (scale, sustain, or sunset).</li>
  <li><strong>Build or buy.</strong> If the district already licenses a service management platform, a homegrown intake tool is the kind of request this pipeline would redirect.</li>
  <li><strong>One maintainer.</strong> A tool kept up by one faculty member on top of a full teaching load is a risk in itself.</li>
</ul>

<!-- prd-req:q -->
<h3>Questions to settle before the next build</h3>
<ol>
<li>Where should a submitted request be stored so that members of the domain can read it, given that the prototype saves it only in the submitter’s own browser, and who decides whether that is built on the prototype or configured in a district platform?</li>
<li>Which version should a developer build for the assessment and the answers: the six dimensions with four request answers and five inventory answers in section 4, or the revised model in section 10 with gates, added dimensions and added dispositions?</li>
<li>Is the assessment a written record of a conversation with no calculated result, or does the product calculate a result, as the calibration step in section 7 and the success measure in section 8 imply?</li>
<li>How does an inventory record get into the product: who enters it, through what screen, and what are the allowed values for category, license, and how it is reported to be working?</li>
<li>Where is the one measure and its current baseline captured before a request can become a pilot, and what time limit counts as completing the form in a few minutes?</li>
</ol>
<!-- /prd-req:q -->

<h2>11. Roadmap</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>September 2026.</strong> Working draft started. Model reviewed by district information technology.</li>
  <li><strong>Fall 2026.</strong> One criteria set. Model revised. Calibration on past requests.</li>
  <li><strong>After calibration.</strong> Inventory round with the colleges, then the first real requests.</li>
  <li><strong>Before any launch.</strong> Decide the platform, and add sign-in, access control, and an audit trail.</li>
</ul>

